{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788317206,"nonce":"QmnSwN3v/KYgHP8tjlmB4nRmLPY=","message":"Found in play, m1-20260901. Evan, watching the window: *\"do you not see messages like 'chess table needs chairs'? if not file an issue.\"*\n\nI saw it. `Alert_ChessTableNoChairs` was in `digest.alerts.active` and printed in **every day-boundary snapshot from day 10 to day 23** — thirteen in-game days — and I never acted on it. The honest finding is not that the alert is invisible; it is that **nothing in the loop turns \"an alert is active\" into \"somebody decided about it.\"**\n\nThe cause was mine and it was a layout bug: my rec-hall IR put the chess table at (107,121) and clustered all three stools around the dining table at (109,120)-(110,119), so the chess table had no sittable cell adjacent. Two `build {def:\"Stool\"}` calls at (107,120) and (106,121) fixed it. Thirteen days of a joy building that could not be used, in the room built to satisfy T5.\n\n\n- **`turn.md`'s trust table answers a different question.** It is a superb list of *which alerts to believe and how far* — `Alert_NeedDefenses` never, `Alert_ColonistNeedsTend` inverts, armament has no alert at all. It says nothing about alerts not on the list, and `Alert_ChessTableNoChairs` is not on it. An alert the table does not mention gets no handling at all.\n- **`triggered.md` is keyed to moments, not to standing conditions.** It fires on threat letters, Zzzt, roster changes, casualties. A chronic Medium alert is not a moment; it just sits.\n- **`daily.md` is a fixed 5-item sweep under a 7-item cap.** It cannot grow an item per alert def, and it should not.\n- **The digest faithfully published it every single time.** The observation surface did its job. The gap is entirely on the decision side.\n- **`alert-mute` is the right shape and only covers the deliberate case.** It exists so a decision not to fix something is recorded with a reason (`280fb78`). What is missing is anything that *notices* an alert has been neither fixed nor muted for N reads. I muted two alerts this run with written reasons and left a third to rot, and no artifact distinguishes those two behaviours.\n\n\nThe cheapest version is a **turn.md trip-wire over the digest the loop already holds**, costing zero extra queries — the same argument that put `food-days-floor` and `power-deficit` there:\n\n\u003e **alert-undecided** — flag any `alerts.active` id that is neither muted nor named in a ledger `action` line within the last N day-boundary reads *(N proposed 3)*. The act is one of three, and all three are cheap: fix it, `alert-mute` it with a reason, or log `ok` with the reading that makes it acceptable. Silence is the failure.\n\nThat turns the existing `digest.alerts.muted` block into half of a two-sided ledger: muted-with-reason on one side, acted-on on the other, and anything in neither is visible by diff. It also composes with `280fb78`'s wake behaviour — an alert that wakes the run repeatedly and is never decided is precisely the noise `alert-mute` was shipped to absorb.\n\n\n1. `checklists/turn.md` gains the item above, with its `flag`, `act`, `why` and `retire-when`, cited from this comment.\n2. The ledger gains whatever is needed to make \"was this alert acted on\" a `grep`, not a judgement — most likely an `alert` field on the `action` line naming the id.\n3. `accept/4.2-play-loop.py` checks it: for each alert id present in three consecutive day digests, assert the run either muted it or logged an action naming it. Run `m1-20260901` **fails that check on `Alert_ChessTableNoChairs`**, which is the regression test written from the failure.\n4. `playbook/` gets the general lesson, since a lesson no checklist cites is half-landed: *the digest publishing a problem is not the same as anyone deciding about it, and a Medium-priority alert is the easiest thing in the world to read thirteen times without acting.*\n\n\nThat the mod should refuse to advance on an undecided alert. `280fb78` already wakes the run on every `alert_on` transition and that is the right amount of interruption; the missing piece is bookkeeping across reads, which is the checklist system's job, not the halt's.","files":null}]}